   "transcript": "Interviewer: So what happened next? To find out I’m with historian Dr Lawrence Goldman of Oxford University. So, the railways. What becomes of them?\nDr Goldman: Well, in 1830 the Liverpool and Manchester Railway is opened and that does something very special. It becomes commonplace for people to move as passengers and as they move, so space changes. It constricts time changes. One doesn’t have to devote so much time to moving around. And it also changes Britain. It changes the national culture. Indeed one can use that term, ‘national culture’, really for the first time. It allows us to have a national postal service from 1840, and from the 1850s we have, for example, national newspapers for the first time.\nInterviewer: So for once you get a copy of The Times not two weeks out of date but something which is actually printed the previous day?\nDr Goldman: The previous day, and Victorians would be able to read these newspapers at their breakfast table. They’ve been printed in London the night before and they’ve been delivered, and you can get the news from Westminster, from the centre of the Empire, with your breakfast.\nInterviewer: So what you see in this period is a very intimate relationship between invention, social change leading to greater innovation, is that right?\nDr Goldman: Absolutely. The invention comes and it changes in so many ways the way people think and then the way people behave, and those changes come really quite rapidly. The railway comes and immediately people start to change where they live, the pattern of how they live. Their leisure changes, the way they interact with other people changes, and all of those things then set up new demands, new unfulfilled wants and more entrepreneurs begin to think of new ideas, and more innovations and new inventions follow. It’s a kind of virtuous circle of change producing change.\nInterviewer: When does it all go wrong? Because Britain is at the top of its game but soon it isn’t.\nDr Goldman: Well, from the 1820s to the 1870s we’re often called the workshop of the world, but from the 1870s historians begin to talk about entrepreneurial failure. It looks as if we become complacent. We’ve done it so well in one way that we don’t continue to innovate. We stick with the technologies and the business organisations and the markets that we already have. And in the later 19th century, there’s a Second Industrial Revolution dependent not on coal and iron but on new technologies, on chemicals, on electricals, and that new industrial revolution is taken on elsewhere in Europe, particularly in Germany, and it’s there that the technological impulse and the innovative impulse seems to reside later.\nInterviewer: And that’s why when the next great invention comes along, it is not a British one."
