   "transcript": "Presenter: Superstition has it there must always be ravens in residence at the Tower. They’re related to crows, black like them and clever like them, but they’re twice the size and by royal decree there are always six at the Tower because legend says if the Tower of London ravens are lost or fly away, the Crown will fall and Britain with it. At the moment there are actually seven at the Tower and the man charged with keeping them there and keeping them happy is Chris Skaife, who has the excellent job title of the Ravenmaster. I went to meet him.\n[Example]\nMr Skaife: I’ve been the Ravenmaster here at the Tower of London for six years now. I’ve observed them and what they do, and they all have different personalities. Some of them are nice and quiet, some of them are angry, some of them are funny, playful, naughty. They are indeed very, very intelligent animals.\n[Question 1]\nMr Skaife: They steal stuff, they chase the visitors that we have around here sometimes, trying to get hold of their sandwiches or stuff like that, but it’s quite comical to watch them.\n[Question 2]\nMr Skaife: I get nipped very frequently by them, absolutely yes.\n[Question 3]\nMr Skaife: It hurts an awful lot. I have several scars from the ravens, yes.\nPresenter: Chris is what is commonly known as a Beefeater, although the formal name is Yeoman Warder. He got the job after completing the mandatory 22 years of military service, and it does come with some perks.\n[Question 4]\nMr Skaife: I live within the walls of the Tower of London. There are currently 37 young warders living here, and about 150 people live inside the walls of the Tower of London, so this is our home and this is where I live and my daily working business.\n[Question 5]\nMr Skaife: It is extraordinary, yeah. It’s an honour and a privilege to say that I can live inside, you know, a fabulous iconic building like this. When I first arrived, I lived in a flat next to the Bloody Tower. Can you imagine getting my wife and my daughter to say, you know, ‘Where, where are you going to live? Oh I’m going to live next to the Bloody Tower’.\nInterviewer: Your daughter get (got) into trouble for swearing no doubt at school?\nMr Skaife: Well, I hope not. She was 21 at the time so…\n[Question 6]\nMr Skaife: That’s a very good question. I’d been here, I’d done my story, which is part and parcel of the guided tours that we do. We call it a story and we learn that and we get a mentor to help us learn that and it normally takes about six to eight months to learn it, and during that period of time, the old Ravenmaster Derek Coyle who had been with the ravens, working with the ravens for many many years, he came up to me one day and he said, ‘Boy,’ he says, ‘I think the ravens might like you.’ And I said, ‘How do you know the ravens might like me?’ And he said, ‘Come and meet me tonight,’ he said, ‘I’ll show you.’ So I went down and met him at the cages and he opened up the cages, he says, ‘Get in,’ and I got inside the cage and there was (were) two massive ravens staring at me, and I stood next to this perch and they started to move a little bit closer and a little bit closer to me and he says, ‘Get out quickly.’ So I got out, he said, ‘The ravens like you,’ he says, ‘You’re now on my team.’ So it was the ravens that picked me rather than the other way around.\n[Question 7]\nMr Skaife: My day starts very early in the morning. The ravens sleep at nighttime so they go to bed at last light and they wake up at first light. In the summer months that can be as early as 4.30 in the morning and so I let them out, I go round, say hello to them all, let them out. I prepare their food in my store. Of course the ravens spend all their day out and about so that the public can see them and take photographs of them and enjoy the intelligence, the beauty of the ravens, and then in the evening time I have to round them up, a bit like a shepherd, and put them back into their cages. They know it’s bed time and most of the time they actually put themselves to bed, which is great for me.\nInterviewer: And you don’t have to go through bath time at least?\nMr Skaife: I don’t have to go through bath time as well yeah, which is great."
